  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nThru-Sat Night...Pleasant, dry, cool today. Temperatures below \nnormal, mostly in the 60s, but a few L70s along the Treasure Coast \nand across portions of Okeechobee County this afternoon. Weak \nsurface high pressure across the western Gulf will push toward the \nFL peninsula into Sat, emerging into the western Atlc Sat overnight \ninto Sun. This will further weaken the pressure gradient as our \ncurrent W/NW winds 8-13 mph (occasional higher gusts) become more \nlight/variable thru Sat night, then more S/SE on Sun increasing to 6-\n12 mph in response to an approaching front. Humidity will become \nmore noticeable again on Sun as moisture surges ahead of the \nboundary. Conditions remain dry thru Sun aftn (at least) and a \nless than 20% chance of light showers developing Sun night.\n\nHave opted to keep fog mention out of the grids/zones for\novernight/Sat morning as increasing cloud-cover should inhibit \nany widespread organization of patchy fog, but will allow future \nshifts to take another look in the event some mention is \nnecessary. \n\nHigh temps on Sat mostly in the 60s - low I-4 northward and U60s \nnear Melbourne southward and could see L70s along the immediate St. \nLucie coast and across Martin County. Min readings each overnight \nmainly in the 50s, but might see some U40s across north Lake and NW \nVolusia counties. \n\nSun-Thu...Previous Modified...A mid-level trough and its associated \nsurface front move through the southeast U.S. early next week. The \nfront sags thru the area on Mon while ridging holds over the SW \nAtlc. Weak low pressure developing along the boundary looks to more \ncleanly pull the front south of the area into mid-week, and high \npressure building over the eastern U.S. begins to advect a drier \nairmass Wed/Thu. \n\nDry conditions persist Sun with the next rain chances (20-40%) - \nnorth of the Treasure Coast beginning to move into the area from \nnorth to south Mon, and SCT-NMRS showers (50-70%) persist within \nthe forecast on Tue. Mostly dry conditions then settle into \nWed/Thu, although developing onshore flow may promote a very low \nchance (~10-20%) for coastal showers each day. Temperatures \nranging the U60s (far north) to M70s (south), except U70s for \nMartin County Sun-Tue begin a warming trend Wed, reaching the U70s\nto L80s areawide by Thu.",
